{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>You shall not plow with an ox and a donkey together.</b> The verse speaks of the reality — an ox and a donkey in plowing — and the same law applies to any labor: it is forbidden to yoke two species together to pull the wagon." "<b>And the sage Rabbi Abraham wrote</b> the reason of this commandment: that HaShem had compassion on all His works, for the strength of the donkey is not like the strength of the ox. According to his words, it would be specifically an ox and a donkey and not other animals, but this is not the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory. Rather, the essential reason for this commandment is because of the concern of mixed species (kil'ayim), for perhaps they will come to crossbreeding — and the way of a farmer is to bring his yoke to the barn, and perhaps they will come to crossbreeding and produce divergent offspring, thereby contradicting the act of Creation, in which it is said (Genesis 1) למינה, למינהו — 'after its kind.' And what strengthens this reason is that this verse was placed in the middle between two verses of kil'ayim: the verse before it — לא תזרע כרמך — and the verse after it — לא תלבש שעטנז. Because in the prohibition of kil'ayim we were commanded three things: in the land, in animals, and in garments; therefore He arranged here the three categories of kil'ayim in three consecutive verses, one after the other. And in the order of Kedoshim all three were combined in a single verse, as it says (Leviticus 19): את חקותי תשמרו בהמתך לא תרביע כלאים שדך לא תזרע כלאים ובגד כלאים שעטנז לא יעלה עליך."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:19.148209738Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}